# Backend — Data Models & Persistence

## Required models and fields (do not rename or drop any)
| Model | Fields |
|---|---|
| BusinessProfile | uuid, name, domain, industry, description, competitors (JSON), status, created_at, updated_at |
| PipelineRun | uuid, profile_uuid (FK), status, queries_discovered, queries_scored, tokens_used, error_message, started_at, completed_at |
| DiscoveredQuery | uuid, profile_uuid (FK), run_uuid (FK), query_text, estimated_search_volume, competitive_difficulty, opportunity_score, domain_visible, visibility_position, discovered_at |
| ContentRecommendation | uuid, profile_uuid (FK), query_uuid (FK), content_type, title, rationale, target_keywords (JSON), priority, created_at |

- Files follow the suggested tree: `models/profile.py`, `models/query.py`,
  `models/recommendation.py`. Place `PipelineRun` where it is clearly discoverable and
  export every model from `models/__init__.py` so migrations detect them.

## Keys and relationships
- `uuid` is the primary key on every model (UUID PKs are graded). Generate it server-side.
- Foreign keys reference the parent's `uuid`. Define `relationship()` on both sides with
  sensible cascade so deleting a profile does not leave orphans.
- Keep the schema normalised: no duplicated profile data on child rows beyond the FKs.

## Constraints
- `opportunity_score`: float 0.0–1.0. `competitive_difficulty`: 0–100.
- `visibility_position`: integer or null.
- `content_type` ∈ `blog_post | landing_page | faq`; `priority` ∈ `high | medium | low`.
- Status value sets for BusinessProfile/PipelineRun and how `unknown` visibility is stored
  are open decisions — confirm with the user before fixing them in the schema.
- Timestamps are timezone-aware UTC and serialised as ISO 8601 strings.

## Serialisation
- Each model has one `to_dict()` (or schema) used by every endpoint, so field names in
  JSON always match the spec. Recommendations expose `recommendation_uuid` and
  `target_query_uuid` as named in the spec.

## Migrations
- Schema changes go through Flask-Migrate (Alembic). Never use `db.create_all()` as the
  setup path. Commit the `migrations/` folder.
- Migrations must run cleanly on an empty database (reviewers run it cold).
